  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThis afternoon, a cold front is positioned near I-70 through the \nmiddle of our forecast area. Scattered showers and isolated storms \nwere ongoing near the boundary. Temperatures show a large spread \nacross the area with most locations in the 80s near and north of \nboundary, especially where clouds are limited. However, some \nlocations remain in the 70s where cloud cover and rain have been \nmore dominant. South of the front was much warmer, comparable to the \nlast few days. These locations, mainly near and south of I-35, will \nexperience heat index values above 100 degrees again today. Instability\nhas also been steadily increasing throughout the day, and will \ncontinue to do so south of the front. Thus, there remains at least a\nlow chance for a few severe storms in this area this afternoon and \nevening. Strong winds are the main hazard of concern given inverted V\nsoundings and DCAPE greater than 1000 J/kg. Locally heavy rainfall \ncould also occur with pwats of 2.0-2.2 inches.\n\nCAMs show coverage of storms increasing late this evening and \ntonight as large scale ascent increases ahead of a shortwave aloft. \nStorms may move over some of the same areas with 850-300mb flow \nparallel to the cold front. Pwats will remain high so heavy rainfall\nwill become the main concern, especially with training convection. \nHREF shows pockets of 2.0 to 3.0 inches of QPF along and south of \nI-70, and a 70 percent chance of at least 1.0 inch of rain near I-35.\nStill, the risk for flooding appears fairly low given the lack of \nrecent precipitation. Three hour FFG is around 3.0 inches area-wide, \nso any localized flooding will likely be confined to urban areas \nthat see heavy rainfall rates or multiple storms throughout the \nnight.\n\nRain moves south along the front Thursday morning. Low clouds will \nclear from north to south, leaving sunshine and light winds. \nTemperatures will finally be near average instead of near records \narea-wide tomorrow afternoon, with highs in the low to mid 80s! Temps\ncreep up slightly on Friday with stronger southerly flow. POPs then \nincrease this weekend, but uncertainty remains on whether a complex \nof storms develop near another sfc boundary like long range and \nensemble models show Saturday morning. Shorter range models, like the\nNAM, remain dry. A mid-level ridge builds in from the south, and \neventually expands toward the southeastern CONUS. Long range models \nshow a shortwave in the west/southwest flow north of the ridge Sunday\nafternoon, and a sfc front draped across northeastern KS early next \nweek. That set up is keeping 30 to 60 percent POPs in the forecast \nthrough at least early next week.",
